# REFER: junegunn/vim-slash is a great-looking plugin!
# - I only wish my Vim scripts could be so clean and concise!!
# - vim-slash "automatically clears search highlight when cursor is
#   moved", and it modifies star-search to highlight "without moving".
#   - Except the modified star-search *does* scroll the window! It uses
#     the naïve approach — It starts a /- or ?-search, and then runs the
#     opposite command (? or /) to match backwards. (See this Vim tip
#     on avoiding scroll —
#       https://vim.fandom.com/wiki/Highlight_all_search_pattern_matches
#     ). So ultimately the cursor doesn't move, except it jumps forward,
#     then backward, which scrolls the window. (To not scroll, it would
#     have to manually `let @/ = {}` and `set hlsearch`, rather than
#     just running /-search. Which is an amount of additional work. And
#     would make vim-slash less, uh, *tight*. (For a 124 line file, it
#     shows off a lot of advanced, nifty, crafty Vim techniques. I
#     learned a lot!) Anyway, for a non-scrolling stay-put start-search,
#     see vim-blinky-search.)
# - vim-slash also includes an optional match-blink feature, akin to
#   die_blinkënmatchen.vim, but a lot more concise,
# CALSO: vim-slash was inspired by a deprecated plugin:
# - *Improved /-search (experimental)*
#   https://github.com/junegunn/vim-oblique
# - And vim-obliquie has inspired at least one other plugin:
#   - *Automatically clears search highlight*
#     https://github.com/pgdouyon/vim-evanesco
#   Which is where I (thankfully!) spied upon the vim-slash blink feature.
# GROUP: visual_search
[junegunn/opt/vim-slash]
lib = remote_set starter https://github.com/junegunn/vim-slash.git
skip = mr_exclusive !vim_active

